dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A term infant takes her first breaths. Within minutes the flap of the foramen ovale closes functionally.{FIG}Which of the following best explains this closure?",
choices=["Falling prostaglandin E₂ constricts the flap","Left atrial pressure rises above right atrial pressure","Oxygen directly contracts the septum primum","Right atrial pressure rises above left atrial pressure","The ductus venosus closes and pulls the flap shut"], key=1,
exp="In the fetus, oxygenated blood from the placenta returns through the umbilical vein and ductus venosus to the right atrium; about a third crosses the <b>foramen ovale</b> to the left atrium (right atrial pressure is higher). At the first breath the lungs expand, <b>pulmonary vascular resistance falls</b>, pulmonary blood flow and <b>pulmonary venous return to the left atrium rise</b>, and loss of the placenta raises systemic resistance. <b>Left atrial pressure now exceeds right</b>, pushing the flap (septum primum) against the septum secundum; it seals anatomically by 6–12 months (fossa ovalis). The ductus arteriosus closes functionally within about 24 hours from rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The foramen ovale closes at birth because left atrial pressure exceeds right atrial pressure after pulmonary resistance falls.",
wrong={0:"Falling prostaglandins and rising oxygen close the ductus arteriosus, not the foramen ovale.",
2:"Oxygen constricts the ductus, not the atrial septum.",
3:"This is the fetal pressure relationship that keeps the foramen open.",
4:"Ductus venosus closure does not mechanically close the foramen ovale."},
eli5="When the lungs open, lots more blood comes back into the left atrium, so its pressure beats the right side and slams the flap door shut."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A healthy term newborn's ductus arteriosus closes functionally within the first day of life.{FIG}Which pair of changes best explains this closure?",
choices=["Falling oxygen tension and falling bradykinin","Falling oxygen tension and rising prostaglandin E₂","Rising oxygen tension and falling prostaglandin E₂","Rising oxygen tension and rising prostaglandin E₂","Rising pulmonary vascular resistance and rising nitric oxide"], key=2,
exp="In the fetus, low oxygen tension and high circulating <b>prostaglandin E₂</b> (from the placenta, cleared poorly by the fluid-filled lungs) keep the ductus open, diverting right ventricular output from the pulmonary artery to the aorta. After birth, <b>rising oxygen tension</b> constricts ductal smooth muscle and <b>prostaglandin E₂ falls</b> (placenta gone, lungs clear it). Hence: <b>indomethacin or ibuprofen</b> (cyclooxygenase (COX) inhibitors) close a persistent ductus, and <b>prostaglandin E₁</b> keeps it open in ductal-dependent lesions.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The ductus closes because oxygen rises and prostaglandin E₂ falls; nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) close it and prostaglandin E₁ (PGE₁) keeps it open.",
wrong={0:"Oxygen rises after birth.",
1:"Rising prostaglandin E₂ (PGE₂) keeps the ductus open.",
3:"Rising PGE₂ would oppose closure.",
4:"Pulmonary resistance falls after birth."},
eli5="Oxygen squeezes the ductus shut, and the prostaglandin that kept it open disappears once the placenta is gone."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_vsdGross', cap="Autopsy specimen of the left ventricle and outflow tract",
stem="An autopsy specimen from a 4-month-old infant who died of heart failure is shown.{FIG}Which statement about this type of congenital lesion is most accurate?",
choices=["It is the most common congenital heart defect","It is usually cyanotic from birth","Large defects produce louder murmurs than small ones","Most muscular defects require surgery","Most occur in the muscular septum"], key=0,
exp="The <b>ventricular septal defect</b> is the <b>most common congenital heart defect</b> (about 31–42% of congenital heart disease, depending on the source). About <b>80% are membranous</b> (perimembranous, just below the aortic valve, as here) and 20% muscular. It shunts <b>left to right</b> (acyanotic). Loudness does not reflect size: a <b>small</b> ventricular septal defect (VSD) has a high-velocity jet and is often <b>louder</b> and higher-pitched; a large one is lower-pitched and often softer. Small defects, especially muscular ones, often close within about 2 years; large ones cause heart failure and are repaired.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> VSD is the commonest congenital heart defect; most are membranous; small defects are loud and often close spontaneously.",
wrong={1:"A VSD shunts left to right and is acyanotic unless Eisenmenger syndrome develops.",
2:"Small defects are often louder because of the high-velocity jet.",
3:"Many small muscular defects close on their own.",
4:"About 80% are membranous."},
eli5="A hole between the ventricles is the most common heart defect babies are born with. Small holes make a loud noise and often close by themselves."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 7-week-old boy has poor feeding, sweating with feeds, tachypnea and poor weight gain. He was well at birth, when no murmur was heard. A grade 3/6 holosystolic murmur is now heard at the left lower sternal border. Echocardiography shows a large perimembranous ventricular septal defect.{FIG}Which of the following best explains why symptoms appeared at several weeks rather than at birth?",
choices=["Closure of the ductus arteriosus at 6 weeks","Falling pulmonary resistance enlarging the shunt","Maternal antibodies protecting the myocardium at birth","Rising pulmonary vascular resistance reversing the shunt","The defect enlarges after birth"], key=1,
exp="At birth, pulmonary vascular resistance is still high, so little blood crosses a VSD from left to right. Over the <b>first 4–8 weeks pulmonary resistance falls</b>, and the <b>left-to-right shunt increases</b>: the lungs are flooded (tachypnea, pulmonary edema), the left heart is volume-overloaded, and the infant develops <b>heart failure</b> — poor feeding, sweating with feeds, failure to thrive, frequent respiratory infections. Large defects are repaired; untreated, they lead to pulmonary hypertension and eventually Eisenmenger syndrome.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Large left-to-right shunts become symptomatic at 4–8 weeks as pulmonary vascular resistance falls and the shunt grows.",
wrong={0:"The ductus closes within days and is not the cause.",
2:"Maternal antibodies do not protect the myocardium from volume overload.",
3:"Rising resistance with shunt reversal is Eisenmenger syndrome, years later.",
4:"Ventricular septal defects (VSDs) do not enlarge after birth; small ones often close."},
eli5="At birth the lungs still resist flow, so not much blood leaks across the hole. Over the next weeks the lungs relax, more blood pours through, and the baby goes into heart failure."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_asdvsd', cap="Autopsy specimen of the heart with two defects marked by arrows",
stem="The heart of a 61-year-old woman who died of a stroke is shown at autopsy. The upper arrow marks a defect in the interatrial septum that had never been diagnosed.{FIG}If this atrial defect had been large, which arrhythmia would she most likely have developed in adulthood?",
choices=["Atrial fibrillation or flutter","Brugada-type ventricular fibrillation","Complete heart block","Mobitz type II block","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia"], key=0,
exp="An <b>atrial septal defect</b> (upper arrow; lower arrow a muscular ventricular septal defect (VSD)) shunts left to right and volume-overloads the right atrium and ventricle. Atrial septal defects (ASDs) are often diagnosed late; large ones cause <b>atrial dilation with supraventricular tachycardia, atrial flutter and atrial fibrillation</b> in adulthood, right heart failure, and <b>paradoxical embolism</b> (a venous clot crossing to the systemic circulation, causing stroke). Exam: a fixed split S2 and a soft pulmonic flow murmur. Closure by a catheter Amplatzer septal occluder or surgery.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Large ASDs cause atrial arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation (AF), flutter) and paradoxical embolism in adults.",
wrong={1:"Brugada syndrome is a sodium channelopathy, unrelated to ASD.",
2:"Complete heart block is more typical of ostium primum or atrioventricular canal defects and congenital corrected transposition, not a secundum ASD.",
3:"Mobitz II reflects His–Purkinje disease, not atrial volume overload.",
4:"Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia (VT) arises from ventricular scar, not atrial stretch."},
eli5="A hole between the atria makes the right atrium overfilled and stretched for decades, and stretched atria tend to go into atrial fibrillation."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_asdEcg', cap="12-lead electrocardiogram",
stem="A 34-year-old woman has exertional dyspnea and palpitations. A fixed split second heart sound and a soft systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border are heard. Her electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which type of atrial septal defect is most likely?",
choices=["Coronary sinus defect","Ostium primum","Ostium secundum","Patent foramen ovale","Sinus venosus defect"], key=2,
exp="The ECG shows <b>right axis deviation</b> and an <b>incomplete right bundle branch block</b> (rSR′ in V1) from right ventricular volume overload. With a fixed split S2 and a pulmonic flow murmur (extra flow across the pulmonic valve, not across the hole), this is an <b>ostium secundum ASD</b> — about <b>80%</b> of atrial septal defects (ASDs), usually isolated, in the fossa ovalis region. An <b>ostium primum</b> defect (part of the endocardial cushion spectrum, associated with Down syndrome) characteristically gives <b>left axis deviation</b>. A patent foramen ovale is a failure of fusion, not a true ASD, and causes no shunt-related ECG changes.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Ostium secundum ASD (80%) gives right axis deviation with incomplete right bundle branch block (RBBB); ostium primum gives left axis deviation.",
wrong={0:"Coronary sinus (unroofed) defects are rare.",
1:"Ostium primum defects give left axis deviation and are associated with Down syndrome and cleft mitral valve.",
3:"A patent foramen ovale (PFO) causes no significant shunt or ECG changes.",
4:"Sinus venosus defects are less common and associated with anomalous right pulmonary venous drainage."},
eli5="The common kind of hole between the atria overfills the right ventricle, which shows on the ECG as a right-pointing axis and a partial right bundle block."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_pfo', cap="Autopsy specimen of the interatrial septum, viewed from the right atrium, with and without a probe",
stem="A 38-year-old man develops sudden right-sided weakness and aphasia during a long-haul flight. He has a swollen, tender left calf. Carotid imaging and rhythm monitoring are normal. A specimen showing the lesion found on his echocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following tests best demonstrates the mechanism of his stroke?",
choices=["Agitated-saline bubble study","Carotid angiography","Coronary CT angiography","Holter monitoring for 48 hours","Lumbar puncture"], key=0,
exp="A probe passing through the flap of the fossa ovalis is a <b>patent foramen ovale</b> — a failure of fusion of septum primum and secundum, present in about 25% of adults. A <b>deep venous thrombosis</b> (long flight, swollen calf) can cross it to the left atrium when right atrial pressure transiently exceeds left (coughing, straining, Valsalva release) — a <b>paradoxical embolism</b> causing stroke. An <b>agitated-saline (bubble) echo</b> shows bubbles crossing to the left atrium, especially on release of Valsalva. Selected young patients with cryptogenic stroke benefit from patent foramen ovale (PFO) closure.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Paradoxical embolism through a PFO or atrial septal defect (ASD) is shown by an agitated-saline bubble study.",
wrong={1:"Carotid imaging is already normal.",
2:"Coronary CT does not show an interatrial shunt.",
3:"Rhythm monitoring looks for atrial fibrillation, already excluded.",
4:"Lumbar puncture has no role in this stroke."},
eli5="A leg clot traveled to the heart and slipped through a little flap door between the atria into the left side, then up to the brain. Bubbles injected in a vein show the door."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_pda', cap="Autopsy specimen of the aorta and pulmonary artery",
stem="A 3-day-old girl born at 28 weeks' gestation with respiratory distress syndrome has worsening respiratory status, bounding pulses, a widened pulse pressure and a continuous machinery-like murmur. The lesion responsible, in a similar infant at autopsy, is shown (arrow).{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate initial drug therapy?",
choices=["Alprostadil (prostaglandin E₁)","Digoxin","Indomethacin","Propranolol","Sildenafil"], key=2,
exp="A continuous \"machinery\" murmur with <b>bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure</b> in a <b>preterm</b> infant with respiratory distress syndrome is a <b>patent ductus arteriosus</b> (aorta → pulmonary artery, left-to-right, flooding the lungs and stealing systemic diastolic flow). It persists in preterm infants because the immature ductus responds poorly to oxygen and prostaglandin levels stay high. Close it with a <b>cyclooxygenase (COX) inhibitor — intravenous indomethacin (or ibuprofen)</b>, which lowers prostaglandin E₂; catheter closure or surgical ligation if that fails. Prostaglandin E₁ (PGE₁) does the opposite (keeps the ductus open in ductal-dependent lesions).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A symptomatic patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) in a preterm infant is closed with indomethacin or ibuprofen (prostaglandin synthesis inhibition).",
wrong={0:"PGE₁ keeps the ductus open; it is used for ductal-dependent cyanotic or left-sided obstructive lesions.",
1:"Digoxin does not close the ductus.",
3:"Propranolol is for tet spells or hemangiomas, not PDA closure.",
4:"Sildenafil lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and would increase the shunt."},
eli5="A vessel that should close after birth stayed open in this premature baby, sending too much blood to her lungs. An anti-prostaglandin drug makes it close."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 6-week-old infant has bilateral cataracts, sensorineural hearing loss and a continuous machinery-like murmur. The mother had a rash with fever and lymphadenopathy in the first trimester and had never been vaccinated.{FIG}Which of the following cardiac defects is most likely in this infant?",
choices=["Coarctation of the aorta","Ebstein anomaly","Patent ductus arteriosus","Tetralogy of Fallot","Transposition of the great arteries"], key=2,
exp="Cataracts, sensorineural deafness and congenital heart disease after a first-trimester rash is <b>congenital rubella</b>. Its characteristic cardiac lesions are <b>patent ductus arteriosus</b> and <b>peripheral pulmonary artery stenosis</b> (septal defects also occur). Prevention is measles–mumps–rubella (MMR) vaccination before pregnancy. Other exposure associations: maternal diabetes → transposition (and truncus, tricuspid atresia, ventricular septal defect (VSD), infant hypertrophic cardiomyopathy); lithium → Ebstein anomaly; fetal alcohol → septal defects.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Congenital rubella causes patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) and pulmonary artery stenosis with cataracts and deafness.",
wrong={0:"Coarctation is associated with Turner syndrome.",
1:"Ebstein anomaly is associated with maternal lithium.",
3:"Tetralogy is associated with 22q11 deletion and fetal alcohol, among others.",
4:"Transposition is associated with maternal diabetes."},
eli5="Rubella in early pregnancy causes the triad of cataracts, deafness and a heart defect — classically a ductus that stays open."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_clubbing', cap="Photograph of the hands",
stem="A 27-year-old woman who emigrated as an adult was told as a child that she had a \"hole in the heart\" that was never repaired. She now has exertional dyspnea and cyanosis of the lips. Her hands are shown. Hematocrit is 64%, and oxygen saturation is 82% on room air. Her previously loud murmur has become soft, and P2 is loud.{FIG}Which of the following best explains her current findings?",
choices=["Closure of the ventricular defect with right heart failure","Endocarditis of the defect with emboli","Fixed pulmonary hypertension reversing the shunt","Iron deficiency anemia from chronic hypoxia","Shunt flow increasing from left to right"], key=2,
exp="Digital <b>clubbing</b>, central cyanosis and <b>secondary polycythemia</b> in an adult with an unrepaired left-to-right shunt is <b>Eisenmenger syndrome</b>: years of excess pulmonary flow remodel the pulmonary arterioles, <b>pulmonary hypertension becomes fixed</b>, right ventricular pressure rises above left, and the <b>shunt reverses</b> to right-to-left. The murmur softens (less pressure gradient across the defect) and P2 is loud. At this point <b>closing the defect no longer helps</b> and is dangerous; management is pulmonary vasodilators and, ultimately, heart–lung transplantation. Pregnancy carries very high maternal mortality.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Eisenmenger syndrome: an unrepaired left-to-right shunt causes fixed pulmonary hypertension and shunt reversal, with late cyanosis, clubbing and polycythemia.",
wrong={0:"A closing defect would reduce symptoms and not cause cyanosis.",
1:"Endocarditis causes fever and emboli, not chronic cyanosis with polycythemia.",
3:"Chronic hypoxia causes polycythemia, not anemia.",
4:"Increasing left-to-right flow would not cause cyanosis."},
eli5="Years of extra blood flow damaged the lung vessels so badly that the pressure on the right side climbed above the left. Now blue blood flows backward across the hole."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 30-year-old man with a large patent ductus arteriosus that was never closed has developed Eisenmenger syndrome.{FIG}Which pattern of cyanosis would most likely be seen on examination?",
choices=["Blue fingers and toes equally","Blue fingers, pink toes","Blue lips only, with pink extremities","Pink fingers, blue toes","No cyanosis because the ductus has closed"], key=3,
exp="In Eisenmenger syndrome from a <b>patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</b>, deoxygenated blood from the pulmonary artery reverses through the ductus into the aorta <b>beyond the origins of the arm vessels</b> (the ductus joins the aorta distal to the left subclavian artery). The upper body receives oxygenated left ventricular blood (<b>pink fingers</b>), while the lower body gets mixed blood (<b>blue, clubbed toes</b>) — <b>differential cyanosis</b>. A reversed ventricular septal defect (VSD) or atrial septal defect (ASD) causes uniform cyanosis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A reversed PDA causes differential cyanosis (blue toes, pink fingers) because it enters the aorta distal to the arm vessels.",
wrong={0:"Uniform cyanosis occurs with reversed intracardiac shunts (VSD, ASD).",
1:"This is the opposite pattern (seen in transposition with a reversed ductus).",
2:"Central cyanosis would accompany extremity cyanosis, not spare it.",
4:"Eisenmenger requires a persistent ductus; it has not closed."},
eli5="The backward-flowing blue blood joins the aorta after the arteries to the arms have branched off, so only the legs and toes turn blue."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_boot', cap="Chest radiograph, posteroanterior view",
stem="A 3-month-old boy has episodes of cyanosis when he cries or feeds. A harsh systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border. Oxygen saturation is 84% and does not rise with 100% oxygen. His chest radiograph is shown.{FIG}Which feature of his lesion most determines how cyanotic he is?",
choices=["Severity of outflow obstruction","Degree of aortic override","Number of muscular septal defects","Severity of right ventricular hypertrophy","Size of the patent foramen ovale"], key=0,
exp="An upturned apex with a concave pulmonary segment — the <b>boot-shaped heart</b> (\"a typical board question\") — with decreased pulmonary markings, in a cyanotic infant, is <b>tetralogy of Fallot</b>: <b>ventricular septal defect (VSD) + pulmonary (right ventricular outflow tract) stenosis + right ventricular hypertrophy + overriding aorta</b>. It is the commonest cause of cyanosis in early childhood. The <b>degree of pulmonary/right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) stenosis</b> decides how much deoxygenated blood is forced right to left across the VSD into the aorta, and therefore how blue the child is (and when repair is needed). The murmur comes from the stenosis, not the VSD.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In tetralogy of Fallot, the severity of right ventricular outflow obstruction determines the degree of right-to-left shunting and cyanosis.",
wrong={1:"The override allows mixing, but outflow obstruction drives the shunt.",
2:"Tetralogy has one large malalignment VSD.",
3:"right ventricular hypertrophy is a consequence of the obstruction, not the determinant of cyanosis.",
4:"A patent foramen ovale (PFO) is not part of tetralogy and does not determine its cyanosis."},
eli5="The tighter the pulmonary outflow, the more blue blood is pushed across the hole into the aorta, and the bluer the baby."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 14-month-old boy with uncorrected tetralogy of Fallot becomes agitated and deeply cyanotic after a blood draw and breathes rapidly. His parents are taught to place him in the knee-to-chest position during such episodes.{FIG}How does this position relieve the episode?",
choices=["It decreases pulmonary vascular resistance","It decreases systemic venous return","It dilates the right ventricular outflow tract directly","It increases heart rate through the baroreflex","It raises systemic resistance, cutting the shunt"], key=4,
exp="A <b>tet spell</b>: crying, feeding or agitation <b>lowers systemic vascular resistance</b> (and raises pulmonary resistance and infundibular spasm), so more deoxygenated blood shunts right to left across the ventricular septal defect (VSD) — profound cyanosis and hyperpnea. The <b>knee-to-chest position</b> (or squatting in older children) kinks the femoral arteries and <b>raises systemic vascular resistance</b>, pushing blood back through the pulmonary outflow to the lungs. Other treatments: oxygen, morphine (calms, reduces infundibular spasm), intravenous fluids, phenylephrine (raises systemic vascular resistance (SVR)) and a beta-blocker (relaxes the infundibulum).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Knee-to-chest positioning (squatting) raises systemic vascular resistance, reducing right-to-left shunting during tet spells.",
wrong={0:"The position does not lower pulmonary resistance; raising systemic resistance is the mechanism.",
1:"Squatting increases, not decreases, venous return.",
2:"Positioning does not dilate the outflow tract.",
3:"Heart rate changes are not the mechanism."},
eli5="Folding the knees to the chest squeezes the leg arteries, raising pressure on the body side, so blood gets pushed back toward the lungs instead of across the hole."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="In 1944 a surgeon operated on a cyanotic child with severe pulmonary outflow obstruction, working from techniques developed in the laboratory by his technician. A systemic artery was connected to a pulmonary artery.{FIG}What was the goal of this Blalock–Taussig shunt?",
choices=["To close the ventricular septal defect","To send more blood flow to the lungs","To lower right ventricular pressure by venting it","To reduce left ventricular afterload","To redirect venous return directly to the lungs"], key=1,
exp="In <b>tetralogy of Fallot</b> with severe outflow obstruction, too little blood reaches the lungs. The <b>Blalock–Taussig shunt</b> connects a <b>subclavian artery to a pulmonary artery</b>, creating a systemic-to-pulmonary shunt that <b>increases pulmonary blood flow</b> and oxygenation — a palliative bridge (the modified BT shunt uses a graft). It was developed by Alfred Blalock, Helen Taussig and the laboratory technician Vivien Thomas (the film <i>Something the Lord Made</i>). Today most children have complete repair in infancy, timed by the stenosis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The Blalock–Taussig shunt (subclavian to pulmonary artery) palliates cyanosis by increasing pulmonary blood flow.",
wrong={0:"The shunt does not close the ventricular septal defect (VSD); complete repair does.",
2:"It does not vent the right ventricle.",
3:"Left ventricular afterload is not the problem in tetralogy.",
4:"Routing venous return directly to the lungs is the Glenn and Fontan approach for single ventricles."},
eli5="If not enough blood gets to the lungs, hook a body artery straight to the lung artery to send more there."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A newborn girl with tetralogy of Fallot also has hypocalcemic seizures on day 3, a cleft palate and an absent thymic shadow on chest radiography.{FIG}Which of the following embryologic abnormalities best explains her heart defect?",
choices=["Anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular septum","Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse","Failure of the septum primum to fuse with the septum secundum","Persistence of the left superior vena cava","Underdevelopment of the left ventricle"], key=0,
exp="Hypocalcemia (parathyroid aplasia), absent thymus (T-cell deficiency), cleft palate and a conotruncal heart defect is <b>DiGeorge (22q11.2 deletion) syndrome</b>, a defect of <b>third and fourth pharyngeal pouch and neural crest</b> development. Tetralogy of Fallot results from <b>anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular (outlet) septum</b> — a neural crest-dependent process — producing the malaligned ventricular septal defect (VSD), outflow stenosis and overriding aorta. 22q11 is also associated with <b>truncus arteriosus</b> and interrupted aortic arch (TBX1).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Tetralogy of Fallot arises from anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular septum and is associated with 22q11 deletion (DiGeorge).",
wrong={1:"Endocardial cushion failure causes atrioventricular septal defects (Down syndrome).",
2:"Failure of fusion of septum primum and secundum is a patent foramen ovale.",
3:"A persistent left superior vena cava (SVC) drains to the coronary sinus and is usually benign.",
4:"Left ventricular underdevelopment is hypoplastic left heart syndrome."},
eli5="In 22q11 deletion, the cells that build the heart's outflow wall migrate wrongly, so the wall sits too far forward. That one shift makes all four parts of tetralogy."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_tga', cap="Schematic of the circulation in a newborn with a congenital heart lesion, with a normal heart for comparison",
stem="A term boy born to a mother with poorly controlled pregestational diabetes becomes deeply cyanotic within 6 hours of birth. Oxygen saturation is 65% and does not change with 100% oxygen. There is no murmur. Chest radiography shows a narrow superior mediastinum. Echocardiography confirms the anatomy shown, with an intact ventricular septum.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate drug therapy?",
choices=["Furosemide","Indomethacin","Inhaled nitric oxide","Prostaglandin E₁","Propranolol"], key=3,
exp="The aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left: <b>transposition of the great arteries</b>, which creates <b>two parallel circuits</b>. Life depends on <b>mixing</b> — a ventricular septal defect (VSD) (stable), or a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) and patent foramen ovale (unstable). With an intact septum, cyanosis appears within hours and worsens as the ductus closes. Give <b>prostaglandin E₁</b> immediately to keep the ductus open, and perform a <b>balloon atrial septostomy</b> to enlarge the atrial communication if mixing is still poor; definitive repair is the <b>arterial switch</b> in the first weeks. Clues: maternal diabetes, \"egg on a string\" chest film, no murmur.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Transposition with an intact septum needs prostaglandin E₁ (PGE₁) (± balloon atrial septostomy) to maintain mixing, then an arterial switch.",
wrong={0:"Diuresis does not address the lack of mixing.",
1:"Indomethacin would close the ductus and kill the infant.",
2:"Nitric oxide lowers pulmonary resistance but does not create mixing between parallel circuits.",
4:"Propranolol is for tet spells."},
eli5="Blue blood goes round and round the body and red blood goes round and round the lungs. Keep the ductus open with prostaglandin so the two can mix."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_circum', cap="Photograph of a newborn's face",
stem="A 1-day-old boy is noted to look dusky; his face is shown. His tongue is bluish. Breath sounds are clear and equal, and he has no respiratory distress. Pre-ductal oxygen saturation is 78% on room air and 80% after 10 minutes on 100% oxygen.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
choices=["Chest physiotherapy and suctioning","Urgent bedside echocardiography","Observation; this is normal acrocyanosis","Surfactant administration","Trial of continuous positive airway pressure"], key=1,
exp="<b>Central cyanosis</b> (lips, tongue, circumoral) in a newborn with clear lungs and no respiratory distress points to <b>cyanotic congenital heart disease</b>. The <b>hyperoxia test</b> confirms it: a lung problem improves markedly on 100% oxygen, but a right-to-left cardiac shunt does not (the shunted blood never meets the oxygen). The next step is <b>echocardiography</b>; if a ductal-dependent lesion is suspected, start <b>prostaglandin E₁</b> while it is arranged. Acrocyanosis (blue hands and feet with pink lips) is normal in newborns; central cyanosis is not.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Central cyanosis that does not improve with 100% oxygen (failed hyperoxia test) indicates cyanotic heart disease; get an echocardiogram (and consider prostaglandin E₁ (PGE₁)).",
wrong={0:"His lungs are clear; airway clearance will not fix a cardiac shunt.",
2:"Acrocyanosis spares the lips and tongue; central cyanosis is never normal.",
3:"Surfactant treats respiratory distress syndrome in preterm infants, with lung findings.",
4:"Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) treats lung disease; saturation did not improve with oxygen."},
eli5="If a baby's lips are blue and pure oxygen doesn't help, the blood is skipping the lungs inside the heart. Look at the heart with ultrasound."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 5-day-old boy who was well at discharge on day 2 is brought in pale, lethargic and in shock, with weak pulses in all four limbs, a capillary refill of 5 seconds and severe metabolic acidosis. Echocardiography shows a diminutive left ventricle and a tiny ascending aorta, with a closing ductus arteriosus.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate therapy?",
choices=["Cardioversion","Indomethacin","Intravenous epinephrine alone","Oxygen at 100% to close the ductus","Prostaglandin E₁ infusion"], key=4,
exp="In <b>hypoplastic left heart syndrome</b> (tiny left ventricle, mitral and aortic valves), the <b>right ventricle supplies the body through the ductus arteriosus</b>, and pulmonary venous return crosses an atrial septal defect (ASD)/patent foramen ovale (PFO). Babies look well at discharge, then collapse into <b>shock as the ductus closes</b> in the first days (weak pulses everywhere, acidosis). Start <b>prostaglandin E₁</b> at once to reopen the ductus. Definitive management is three-stage single-ventricle surgery (<b>Norwood, Glenn, Fontan</b>) or transplant. High concentrations of oxygen are avoided because they lower pulmonary resistance and steal systemic flow.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Ductal-dependent systemic circulation (hypoplastic left heart, critical coarctation) presents with shock as the ductus closes; give prostaglandin E₁ (PGE₁) immediately.",
wrong={0:"The rhythm is not the problem.",
1:"Indomethacin would close the ductus and worsen shock.",
2:"Inotropes may help, but without reopening the ductus there is no systemic outflow.",
3:"Oxygen would promote ductal closure and pulmonary overcirculation."},
eli5="In this baby the right side has to pump blood to the body through the ductus. When the ductus shut, the body lost its blood supply. Prostaglandin opens it again."),

dict(sec=26, img='G_truncus', cap="Schematic of a normal heart (left) and a congenital heart lesion (right)",
stem="A 2-week-old infant has tachypnea, poor feeding, mild cyanosis, bounding pulses and a harsh systolic murmur with a single loud S2. He also has hypocalcemia and a small thymus. The schematic shows his cardiac anatomy compared with normal.{FIG}Failure of which embryologic structure most directly explains his heart defect?",
choices=["Aorticopulmonary (spiral) septum","Endocardial cushions","Septum primum","Septum secundum","Sinus venosus"], key=0,
exp="A single great vessel arising over a ventricular septal defect (VSD) and supplying the systemic, pulmonary and coronary circulations is <b>persistent truncus arteriosus</b>: the neural crest-derived <b>aorticopulmonary (spiral) septum failed to form</b>, so the truncus never divided into aorta and pulmonary artery. Mixed blood gives mild cyanosis, pulmonary overcirculation gives heart failure, a single truncal valve gives a single S2, and diastolic runoff gives bounding pulses. Hypocalcemia and thymic hypoplasia point to <b>22q11 deletion</b>. Surgical repair in early infancy. (Failure of the septum to <b>spiral</b> produces transposition instead.)<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Truncus arteriosus results from failure of the aorticopulmonary septum (neural crest) to form; it is associated with 22q11 deletion.",
wrong={1:"Endocardial cushion failure causes atrioventricular septal defects.",
2:"Septum primum defects cause ostium primum atrial septal defects (ASDs) or secundum ASDs, not a common trunk.",
3:"Septum secundum defects cause secundum ASDs.",
4:"Sinus venosus abnormalities cause sinus venosus ASDs and anomalous venous drainage."},
eli5="Normally a wall grows down the middle of one big outflow tube to split it into the aorta and the lung artery. Here the wall never formed, so one tube serves both."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 2-day-old girl is cyanotic. Her electrocardiogram shows left axis deviation and tall P waves. Echocardiography shows no tricuspid valve, a hypoplastic right ventricle, an atrial septal defect and a small ventricular septal defect.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Ebstein anomaly","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Tetralogy of Fallot","Total anomalous pulmonary venous return","Tricuspid atresia"], key=4,
exp="<b>Tricuspid atresia</b>: no tricuspid valve and a hypoplastic right ventricle. Systemic venous blood must cross an <b>atrial septal defect (ASD)</b> to the left atrium, and pulmonary flow depends on a <b>ventricular septal defect (VSD) or patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</b>. A cyanotic newborn with <b>left axis deviation</b> (the left ventricle dominates) and tall P waves (right atrial enlargement) is the classic clue — most other cyanotic lesions show right axis deviation. Treatment is staged single-ventricle palliation (Glenn, then Fontan).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Tricuspid atresia is a cyanotic lesion with left axis deviation, requiring an ASD plus a VSD or PDA to survive.",
wrong={0:"Ebstein anomaly has a displaced, not absent, tricuspid valve and massive right atrial enlargement.",
1:"Hypoplastic left heart has a tiny left ventricle and a dominant right ventricle.",
2:"Tetralogy has right axis deviation from right ventricular hypertrophy.",
3:"Total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR) has pulmonary veins draining to the right heart with increased pulmonary markings."},
eli5="No tricuspid valve means the right ventricle never grew, so the left ventricle does all the work. That's why the ECG axis points left in a blue baby."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A newborn's mother took lithium throughout pregnancy for bipolar disorder. The infant is cyanotic, and chest radiography shows massive, box-shaped cardiomegaly with decreased pulmonary markings. The ECG shows a short PR interval with a delta wave.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the cardiac abnormality?",
choices=["Tricuspid leaflets displaced into the right ventricle","Failure of the truncus to divide","Pulmonary veins draining to the right atrium","Single ventricle with a common atrioventricular valve","Thickened, stenotic pulmonary valve leaflets"], key=0,
exp="<b>Ebstein anomaly</b>: the <b>tricuspid leaflets are displaced apically into the right ventricle</b>, \"atrializing\" part of it, causing tricuspid regurgitation and a huge right atrium — massive \"box-shaped\" cardiomegaly with decreased pulmonary markings. Right-to-left flow across an atrial septal defect (ASD)/patent foramen ovale (PFO) causes cyanosis. It is associated with <b>maternal lithium</b> and with <b>Wolff–Parkinson–White</b> accessory pathways (delta wave). Management: repair or replacement of the tricuspid valve, ablation of pathways.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Ebstein anomaly (apically displaced tricuspid leaflets) is associated with maternal lithium and WPW, with box-shaped cardiomegaly.",
wrong={1:"Failure of the truncus to divide is truncus arteriosus.",
2:"Pulmonary veins draining to the right heart is total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR).",
3:"A single ventricle with a common atrioventricular valve is a complex single-ventricle lesion.",
4:"A thickened, stenotic pulmonary valve is pulmonic stenosis (Noonan)."},
eli5="The tricuspid valve sits too low inside the right ventricle, so it leaks and the right atrium balloons. Lithium in pregnancy is the classic link."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 4-month-old girl with upslanting palpebral fissures, a single palmar crease and hypotonia has poor feeding, tachypnea and a holosystolic murmur. Echocardiography shows a large defect at the crux of the heart involving the lower atrial septum and inlet ventricular septum with a single common atrioventricular valve.{FIG}Which embryologic structure failed to develop normally?",
choices=["Aorticopulmonary septum","Endocardial cushions","Septum secundum","Sinus venosus","Truncus arteriosus"], key=1,
exp="Features of <b>Down syndrome (trisomy 21)</b> with a defect at the crux — an ostium primum ASD, an inlet VSD and a common atrioventricular valve — is a complete <b>atrioventricular septal defect</b> (atrioventricular canal), from failure of the <b>endocardial cushions</b> to fuse and divide the atrioventricular canal. It is the characteristic lesion of Down syndrome (which also has ventricular septal defects (VSDs) and atrial septal defects (ASDs)), presenting with heart failure as pulmonary resistance falls and a high risk of early pulmonary vascular disease, so repair is done in infancy. Other Down comorbidities: duodenal atresia, leukemia, early Alzheimer disease.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Atrioventricular septal (endocardial cushion) defects are the characteristic cardiac lesion of Down syndrome.",
wrong={0:"Aorticopulmonary septum defects cause truncus or transposition.",
2:"Septum secundum defects cause secundum ASDs.",
3:"Sinus venosus defects are upper atrial ASDs with anomalous pulmonary veins.",
4:"Persistent truncus arteriosus is a conotruncal defect of 22q11."},
eli5="In Down syndrome, the soft pads in the middle of the heart that should build the center walls and valves don't fuse, leaving one big hole and one shared valve."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 6-year-old girl has an outgoing, overly friendly personality, mild intellectual disability, a broad forehead, full lips and a small upturned nose. She has had hypercalcemia. A systolic ejection murmur is heard at the right upper sternal border, and blood pressure is higher in the right arm than the left.{FIG}Which of the following cardiac lesions is most likely?",
choices=["Atrioventricular septal defect","Coarctation of the aorta","Pulmonic valve stenosis","Supravalvular aortic stenosis","Truncus arteriosus"], key=3,
exp="An \"elfin\" face, outgoing cocktail-party personality, intellectual disability and hypercalcemia is <b>Williams syndrome</b> — a 7q11.23 microdeletion including the <b>elastin</b> gene. Its characteristic lesion is <b>supravalvular aortic stenosis</b> (narrowing just above the aortic valve); peripheral pulmonary artery stenosis also occurs. The jet directed into the right brachiocephalic artery (Coandă effect) can make right arm pressure higher than left.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Williams syndrome (elastin deletion) causes supravalvular aortic stenosis.",
wrong={0:"atrioventricular septal defects are the lesion of Down syndrome.",
1:"Coarctation is associated with Turner syndrome.",
2:"Pulmonic valve stenosis is typical of Noonan syndrome.",
4:"Truncus arteriosus is associated with 22q11 deletion."},
eli5="Williams syndrome is missing the gene for elastin, the stretchy protein in artery walls, so the aorta is narrow just above its valve."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 30-year-old man has an absent thumb on the right hand and a triphalangeal thumb on the left. His father had similar hand anomalies and a pacemaker. Echocardiography shows a secundum atrial septal defect, and his ECG shows first-degree atrioventricular block.{FIG}A mutation in which gene is most likely?",
choices=["FBN1","JAG1","PTPN11","TBX5","TBX1"], key=3,
exp="Upper-limb anomalies (thumb/radial ray) with an <b>atrial septal defect (ASD)</b> (or ventricular septal defect (VSD)) and <b>conduction disease</b>, inherited autosomal dominantly, is <b>Holt–Oram syndrome</b> from mutations in <b>TBX5</b>, a T-box transcription factor. Dr. Gardner's point: the known congenital heart disease genes are mostly <b>transcription factors</b>, probably shifting the timing of heart development (TBX1 in 22q11, NKX2.5, GATA4).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Holt–Oram syndrome (TBX5) combines upper-limb defects with ASD and conduction disease.",
wrong={0:"FBN1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome.",
1:"JAG1 mutations cause Alagille syndrome (pulmonary artery stenosis, bile duct paucity).",
2:"PTPN11 mutations cause Noonan syndrome (pulmonic stenosis).",
4:"TBX1 is the key gene in 22q11 deletion (conotruncal defects)."},
eli5="A family with missing or odd thumbs plus holes between the atria and slow heart wiring: Holt–Oram, from a TBX5 mutation."),
dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 3-day-old boy has tachypnea, mild cyanosis and poor feeding. Oxygen saturation is 86%. Chest radiography shows increased pulmonary vascular markings and a figure-of-eight cardiac silhouette. Echocardiography shows all four pulmonary veins joining a vertical vein that drains into the left brachiocephalic vein, with right-to-left flow across a patent foramen ovale.{FIG}Which of the following is essential for this infant's survival before surgery?",
choices=["A closed ductus arteriosus","A competent tricuspid valve","An interatrial communication","Pulmonary valve stenosis","Systemic hypertension"], key=2,
exp="All pulmonary veins draining to the systemic venous side is <b>total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR)</b>; the supracardiac type (vertical vein to the brachiocephalic vein) gives the <b>‘snowman’</b> (figure-of-eight) silhouette. All venous return — systemic and pulmonary — reaches the right atrium, so the left heart, and therefore the body, receives blood <b>only through an atrial septal defect (ASD) or patent foramen ovale</b>. Mixed blood gives mild cyanosis, and pulmonary overcirculation gives increased markings. Treatment is surgical re-routing of the pulmonary veins to the left atrium; obstructed TAPVR is a surgical emergency.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> TAPVR requires an interatrial communication for survival; the supracardiac type gives a snowman silhouette.",
wrong={0:"Ductal closure is not the survival requirement in TAPVR.",
1:"Tricuspid competence does not supply the left heart.",
3:"Pulmonary stenosis would reduce pulmonary flow and is not required.",
4:"Systemic hypertension plays no role."},
eli5="All the oxygen-rich blood from the lungs goes back to the wrong (right) side. The only way it reaches the body is through a hole between the atria."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 2-month-old girl has a loud, high-pitched grade 3/6 holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border found at a routine visit. She is feeding and growing well, has no tachypnea, and her oxygen saturation is 99%. Echocardiography shows a 2-mm muscular ventricular septal defect with normal chamber sizes and normal pulmonary pressure.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate management?",
choices=["Catheter device closure now","Observe; it will likely close","Prostaglandin E₁ infusion","Start furosemide and digoxin","Surgical closure before 6 months of age"], key=1,
exp="A <b>small muscular VSD</b> is restrictive: a high-velocity jet makes it <b>loud and high-pitched</b>, but the shunt is small, so there is no volume overload, heart failure or pulmonary hypertension. Many small muscular (and some perimembranous) ventricular septal defects (VSDs) <b>close spontaneously within about 2 years</b>; a murmur that gets louder and higher-pitched can mean the hole is getting smaller. <b>Observe</b> with follow-up. Large VSDs with heart failure, failure to thrive or pulmonary hypertension are repaired.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Small, asymptomatic muscular VSDs are observed because most close spontaneously.",
wrong={0:"Device closure is not needed for a small defect that is likely to close.",
2:"Prostaglandin E₁ (PGE₁) is for ductal-dependent lesions.",
3:"She has no heart failure to treat.",
4:"Surgery is for large defects with symptoms or pulmonary hypertension."},
eli5="A tiny hole makes a loud whistle but leaks very little blood, and it usually closes by itself in a year or two. Just keep an eye on it."),

dict(sec=26, img=None,
stem="A 45-year-old woman has exertional dyspnea and atrial flutter. Echocardiography shows an 18-mm ostium secundum atrial septal defect with a left-to-right shunt, right ventricular dilation and normal pulmonary artery pressure. The defect has adequate rims of surrounding septum.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment of the defect?",
choices=["Heart–lung transplantation","No treatment, because the shunt is left to right","Percutaneous catheter device closure","Pulmonary vasodilator therapy alone","Surgical ventricular septal repair"], key=2,
exp="A symptomatic secundum atrial septal defect (ASD) with <b>right ventricular volume overload</b> and <b>normal pulmonary pressure</b> should be closed. A secundum defect with adequate rims is closed <b>percutaneously with a catheter septal occluder (Amplatzer)</b>; surgery is used for primum or sinus venosus defects, large defects or inadequate rims. Closure is avoided once <b>fixed pulmonary hypertension</b> (Eisenmenger physiology) has developed. Atrial arrhythmias may persist after closure.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A secundum ASD with right heart enlargement and normal pulmonary pressures is closed, usually with a percutaneous device.",
wrong={0:"Transplantation is for end-stage Eisenmenger syndrome.",
1:"A left-to-right shunt with right heart enlargement benefits from closure; closure is avoided only once fixed pulmonary hypertension develops.",
3:"Vasodilators treat pulmonary arterial hypertension, which she does not have.",
4:"The defect is atrial, not ventricular."},
eli5="The hole between her atria is overloading the right side. A plug delivered through a leg vein can seal it without open surgery."),
